# Every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs and the deploy
# provides exactly those. `env_file: .env` fan-out — which hands every container the whole estate's
# secrets — is banned, and this file is what makes the alternative possible.
#
# Copy to `.env` for local work. `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- required --------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# This service's own database. It reads no other, and CI fails on a second connection string.
AETHERHOLM_DATABASE_URL=postgres://aetherholm:aetherholm@127.0.0.1:5432/aetherholm

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL and empty here: a development stack is one network. Set the first
# in the estate, where one pod answers for mainnet and testnet and the gateway
# stamps `CF-Network` on every request. See micro-deploy
# `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
AETHERHOLM_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
# ── WHICH ESTATE THIS DEPLOYMENT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED in the estate, and the render sets it for every pod. It was once
# documented here as "never in production" and that was wrong twice over:
#
#   1. The pod keys its own database handle by it. Without it a testnet
#      deployment registers its testnet DSN under the name `mainnet` and then
#      refuses every request stamped `CF-Network: testnet`.
#
#   2. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE CALLS NEVER REACH THE GATEWAY. An outbox relay POSTing
#      to another service goes container to container; nothing stamps a header
#      on it and nothing can. Without a declared network those answer 500.
#
# The header still WINS when present, so this cannot mask a mis-stamped request
# from outside — it only answers the internal callers that never had one. Leave
# it empty only where a pod genuinely serves both estates.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=


# Identity's JWKS and issuer. Token verification is the only thing this service asks identity for.
# The credential worlds presents on POST /v1/provision is verified against this JWKS and must
# carry the `aetherholm:provision` scope — an unauthenticated provisioning endpoint is a
# free-worlds endpoint, and worlds' conformance suite checks the refusal.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4001/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:4001

# HMAC key for outbound event signatures, so a subscriber can prove an event came from us.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# The boot guard checks the SHAPE of the value, not its length: base64 or hex only (a typed value
# has a hyphen or an underscore and is refused however long it is), at least 32 DECODED BYTES, and
# a measured entropy floor that rejects 64 zeros. There is no NODE_ENV exemption and no escape
# hatch — the placeholder that sat in a public compose file was 40 characters and passed every
# service in the estate (micro-org #142). Leaving a placeholder here is refused at boot,
# deliberately. Do not paste one back in.
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# --- optional, with the defaults shown ---------------------------------------------------------

# The secrets POST /v1/events will ACCEPT, comma-separated, newest first. Unset means "only
# OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET", which is what a deploy that has never rotated wants. Set both values for
# the length of a rotation, then drop the old one — that is the whole point of the list, and it is
# what turns a flag day into a service-by-service change.
#
# EVERY ENTRY CLEARS THE SAME BAR AS THE SIGNING KEY. The outgoing one is the key an attacker
# already holds if it leaked, and "just for the drain" is exactly how a placeholder survives the
# rotation meant to remove it.
# OUTBOX_ACCEPT_SECRETS=

# 4120 — the port the registry row in micro-ui pins (`ui/packages/ui/src/surfaces.test.ts`,
# BOUND table). CI fails this file if it disagrees with src/env.ts.
PORT=4120
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
AETHERHOLM_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# Names this replica in jobs.locked_by. Defaults to the hostname, which is the container id under
# compose and the pod name under Kubernetes.
# INSTANCE_ID=

# The image tag, reported on every log line and in the release manifest.
# CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev

# --- not read by this service ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is NO upstream URL and NO service token here, deliberately: phase 1 makes no outbound HTTP
# call. The title contract is inbound — worlds calls this service with its own scoped credential —
# and every background timer is a leased job against this service's own database. A variable
# nothing reads is a secret nothing needed handed to a container anyway.
#
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT and the rest of OTEL_* are read by the OpenTelemetry SDK loaded
# ahead of the process, not by src/env.ts. They belong in the deploy, not here.
#
# AETHERHOLM_TEST_DATABASE_URL is read only by the test suite, and only when its name contains
# "test" — the database-backed tests truncate.
